/* ==========================================================================
   SBAR practice: communicate a prescription error or an immunization
   recommendation (Dr. Lahoud and Dr. Parsons; Week 11 OSCE review, Week 6
   immunizations, Week 2 EOOR and dispensing lab). Items test application and
   discrimination, not recall of the acronym: sort the sentences of a call,
   find the one flaw in a written SBAR, pick the complete SBAR for a case,
   and read the AAFP Table 2 colors to write the Assessment of a vaccine SBAR.
   Sources: APC-III-Week-11-OSCE-Prep_2026.pdf (Drive; SBAR components,
   Background versus Assessment, types of prescribing errors, medication list);
   Immunizations-Pre-Lab.pdf slides 20-23 and the 9/21 transcript marked (T);
   EOOR-Answer-Key.pdf (13 stations); Intro-to-Dispensing.pdf slides 16-39;
   2025-2026 Basic Drug Knowledge List.pdf (controlled substance schedules of
   clonazepam CIV, acetaminophen/codeine CIII, Focalin CII);
   AAFP-Adult-Schedule-2026.pdf (Table 2 colors, Notes, Appendix).
   Two SBAR versions are taught, and each item says which one applies:
   - Prescription-error SBAR (Week 11): Background = clinical facts, who the
     patient is and the prescription; Assessment = the actual problem and why
     it matters. Only a prescribing error needs this call to the prescriber.
   - Vaccine-needs SBAR (Immunizations slide 22): Background = patient
     characteristics and vaccine history; Assessment = what the AAFP schedule
     recommends for that patient.
   Case details (ages, strengths, sigs, dates) are invented for practice and
   do not change any key. Where the sources differ: CII expiration is 30 days
   on slide 22 and "more than 1 month" in the EOOR key; no item keys it.
   Dates are computed against today, 11/21/2026.
   ========================================================================== */
TOPICS.push({id:'sbarl', name:'SBAR: prescription errors', prof:'Dr. Lahoud', open:true, cite:'Week 11 OSCE review; EOOR answer key; Intro to Dispensing slides 16–39', subs:[
  {id:'sort', name:'Sort the sentences of the call', cite:'APC-III-Week-11-OSCE-Prep_2026.pdf (SBAR review: components; Background vs Assessment)'},
  {id:'flaw', name:'Spot the flaw in a written SBAR', cite:'APC-III-Week-11-OSCE-Prep_2026.pdf; Intro-to-Dispensing.pdf slide 39'},
  {id:'case', name:'Case reasoning: error, owner and complete SBAR', cite:'EOOR-Answer-Key.pdf; Intro-to-Dispensing.pdf slides 18–22, 27, 38–39'}]});

TOPICS.push({id:'sbarp', name:'SBAR: immunization recommendations', prof:'Dr. Parsons', open:true, cite:'Immunizations-Pre-Lab.pdf slides 20–23; 9/21 transcript; AAFP 2026 schedule', subs:[
  {id:'sort', name:'Sort the vaccine SBAR', cite:'Immunizations-Pre-Lab.pdf slides 20–22; 9/21 transcript (T)'},
  {id:'rec', name:'Assessment and recommendation from the AAFP tables', cite:'AAFP-Adult-Schedule-2026.pdf Table 2, Notes and Appendix'}]});

QUESTIONS.push(
/* ---- Lahoud: sort the sentences of a prescription-error call ------------ */
{id:'sb01', skill:'apply', type:'match', prof:'Dr. Lahoud', tier:'new', topic:'sbarl', sub:'sort', concept:'sbar-sort-ibuprofen-dose',
 tags:['SBAR','prescribing error','dose too high'], source:'slide',
 stem:'A pharmacist calls a prescriber about an ibuprofen prescription written for 800 mg every 4 hours as needed. Match each sentence of the call to the SBAR part it belongs to.',
 left:'Sentence from the call', right:'SBAR part',
 pairs:[
  {l:'As written, 6 doses of 800 mg in 24 hours is 4.8 g, so the total daily dose prescribed is too high.', r:'Assessment', why:'Week 11 defines the Assessment as the actual problem and why it matters, and its own example is that the total daily dose prescribed is too high. Taken every 4 hours for 24 hours, 800 mg is 6 doses, or 4.8 g. That arithmetic is a conclusion about this prescription, so it is Assessment, not Background.'},
  {l:'I am calling about an ibuprofen prescription you sent to our pharmacy today.', r:'Situation', why:'Week 11 defines the Situation as the reason for the call. This sentence names only the prescription that prompted the call. It gives no patient detail, no clinical fact and no judgment, which keeps the opening short and leaves the facts and the problem for the next two parts.'},
  {l:'I recommend a new prescription with directions that stay at or under 3.2 g/day, such as 800 mg every 8 hours as needed.', r:'Recommendation', why:'Week 11 defines the Recommendation as what should be done to correct the problem. This sentence asks for a new prescription and gives a regimen within the maximum (800 mg every 8 hours is 2.4 g/day). Intro to Dispensing slide 39 corrects a prescribing error by contacting the prescriber.'},
  {l:'The patient is a 46-year-old adult, the prescription reads ibuprofen 800 mg every 4 hours as needed, and the maximum dose of ibuprofen is 3.2 g/day.', r:'Background', why:'Week 11 puts who the patient is, the prescription in question and the clinical facts in the Background, and its own example gives the ibuprofen maximum of 3.2 g/day as Background. This sentence holds the patient, the written directions and the maximum, and draws no conclusion yet.'}],
 teach:'Sort by asking what each sentence does. The Situation names why you are calling. The Background supplies who the patient is, what the prescription says and the clinical fact needed to judge it, such as the usual maximum. The Assessment states the problem that follows from those facts and why it matters, and the Recommendation says what should be done. In a prescription-error SBAR the clinical fact is Background and the conclusion drawn from it is Assessment (Week 11 example: the maximum ibuprofen dose of 3.2 g/day is Background; the total daily dose prescribed is too high is Assessment). The vaccine-needs SBAR differs: there the AAFP recommendation is the Assessment.',
 cite:'APC-III-Week-11-OSCE-Prep_2026.pdf (SBAR review: components; Background vs Assessment); Intro-to-Dispensing.pdf slide 39'},

{id:'sb02', skill:'apply', type:'match', prof:'Dr. Lahoud', tier:'new', topic:'sbarl', sub:'sort', concept:'sbar-sort-cii-refills',
 tags:['SBAR','prescribing error','refills','CII'], source:'slide',
 stem:'A pharmacist calls about a Focalin XR 10 mg prescription for 30 capsules, one capsule every morning, written with 2 refills. Match each sentence of the call to the SBAR part it belongs to.',
 left:'Sentence from the call', right:'SBAR part',
 pairs:[
  {l:'Two refills were prescribed on this schedule II prescription, so the number of refills is inappropriate.', r:'Assessment', why:'This sentence applies the rule to this prescription and names the actual problem, an inappropriate number of refills (Week 11 list of prescribing errors; the EOOR key, station 13: CII cannot have refills). Applying a fact to the case is the Assessment, even though it repeats words from the Background.'},
  {l:'I recommend a new prescription for Focalin XR 10 mg, 30 capsules, with no refills.', r:'Recommendation', why:'The Recommendation says what should be done to correct the problem (Week 11). A new prescription without refills is the specific correction, and the EOOR key, station 13, corrects this error by calling the provider. The sentence is specific enough for the prescriber to answer yes or no.'},
  {l:'The patient is a 24-year-old adult, and Focalin XR 10 mg was prescribed with 2 refills; Focalin XR is a schedule II drug, and schedule II prescriptions are allowed no refills.', r:'Background', why:'The Background holds the patient, the prescription and the clinical facts (Week 11). Focalin is a CS-II drug in the Basic Drug Knowledge list, and Intro to Dispensing slides 22 and 27 give 0 refills for schedule II. The rule is a fact needed to understand the problem, and the conclusion comes next.'},
  {l:'I am calling about a Focalin XR prescription you sent to our pharmacy today.', r:'Situation', why:'The Situation is the reason for the call (Week 11). The sentence names the prescription and nothing else. The patient, the 2 refills and the schedule belong to the Background, and the problem belongs to the Assessment, so adding any of them here would blur the four parts together.'}],
 teach:'Refills are limited by drug category: dangerous drugs have no refill limit, schedule III–V allow up to 5 refills in 6 months, and schedule II allows no refills (Intro to Dispensing slides 22 and 27). Inappropriate refills for a CII is a prescribing error, so the pharmacist calls the prescriber (EOOR key, station 13). In the SBAR, the schedule and the no-refill rule are Background facts; the statement that this prescription carries 2 refills and therefore has an inappropriate number is the Assessment; the Recommendation asks for a new prescription without refills.',
 cite:'Intro-to-Dispensing.pdf slides 22, 27, 38–39; EOOR-Answer-Key.pdf station 13; APC-III-Week-11-OSCE-Prep_2026.pdf'},

{id:'sb03', skill:'apply', type:'match', prof:'Dr. Lahoud', tier:'new', topic:'sbarl', sub:'sort', concept:'sbar-sort-liquid-quantity',
 tags:['SBAR','prescribing error','quantity','liquid'], source:'slide',
 stem:'A pharmacist calls about a sulfamethoxazole/trimethoprim oral suspension prescription, 7.5 mL twice daily for 5 days, written for a quantity of 60 mL. Match each sentence of the call to the SBAR part it belongs to.',
 left:'Sentence from the call', right:'SBAR part',
 pairs:[
  {l:'I recommend a new prescription for 75 mL so that the full 5-day course can be dispensed.', r:'Recommendation', why:'The Recommendation says what should be done to correct the problem (Week 11). A new prescription for 75 mL names the quantity the prescriber should write. A prescribing error is corrected by contacting the prescriber to obtain a new prescription (Intro to Dispensing slide 39).'},
  {l:'The quantity prescribed is insufficient: 60 mL covers only 4 days, so the patient would run out before the course ends.', r:'Assessment', why:'Insufficient prescribed quantity is a prescribing error that is common with liquids (Week 11 list). This sentence states the problem and why it matters, which is that the patient runs out early. 7.5 mL twice daily is 15 mL a day, and 60 mL divided by 15 mL is 4 days.'},
  {l:'I am calling about the quantity on a sulfamethoxazole/trimethoprim suspension prescription you sent today.', r:'Situation', why:'The Situation is the reason for the call (Week 11). This sentence says which prescription and which detail prompted the call, in one line, and it stops before any patient facts or calculation, which are saved for the Background so the listener can follow the order.'},
  {l:'The patient is a 31-year-old adult, and the directions are 7.5 mL twice daily for 5 days, which needs 75 mL, while 60 mL was prescribed.', r:'Background', why:'The Background gives who the patient is, the prescription in question and the clinical facts (Week 11). The directions, the 75 mL that they require (7.5 mL × 2 × 5 days) and the 60 mL written are facts. The conclusion that the quantity is insufficient is left for the Assessment.'}],
 teach:'Insufficient prescribed quantity is the prescribing error that is common with liquid prescriptions (Week 11 list; Intro to Dispensing slide 38). Compute the quantity the directions require: 7.5 mL × 2 doses × 5 days = 75 mL. Those numbers are Background facts. The Assessment states the problem and why it matters: 60 mL lasts 4 days, so the course would end early. The Recommendation requests a new prescription for the needed quantity. The same 60 mL versus 75 mL numbers appear in EOOR station 9 as a dispensing error, where the pharmacy filled less than was prescribed.',
 cite:'APC-III-Week-11-OSCE-Prep_2026.pdf; Intro-to-Dispensing.pdf slides 38–39; EOOR-Answer-Key.pdf station 9'},

{id:'sb04', skill:'apply', type:'match', prof:'Dr. Lahoud', tier:'new', topic:'sbarl', sub:'sort', concept:'sbar-sort-dea-missing',
 tags:['SBAR','prescribing error','DEA number','CIII'], source:'slide',
 stem:'A pharmacist calls about a prescription for Tylenol with Codeine #3 (acetaminophen/codeine) that arrived without the prescriber’s DEA number. Match each sentence of the call to the SBAR part it belongs to.',
 left:'Sentence from the call', right:'SBAR part',
 pairs:[
  {l:'The DEA number is missing, so the prescription is incomplete and cannot be filled until it is supplied.', r:'Assessment', why:'This sentence names the actual problem and why it matters (Week 11). A DEA number required but missing is a prescribing error for CIII–V medications (Week 11 list; EOOR key, stations 6 and 13). The sentence draws the conclusion from the facts, so it is the Assessment.'},
  {l:'I am calling about a Tylenol with Codeine #3 prescription you sent for your patient today.', r:'Situation', why:'The Situation is the reason for the call (Week 11). This sentence identifies the prescription that prompted the call and does not yet describe the patient, the controlled-substance schedule or the missing number, which are the Background and Assessment parts of the call.'},
  {l:'I recommend that you provide your DEA number for this prescription so that we can complete the review and fill it.', r:'Recommendation', why:'The Recommendation says what should be done to correct the problem (Week 11). The EOOR key, stations 6 and 13, gives the correction as contacting the prescriber for the DEA number. The sentence asks for exactly that, which is specific and can be done during the call.'},
  {l:'The patient is a 52-year-old adult, Tylenol with Codeine #3 is a schedule III controlled substance, and a CIII–V prescription must include the prescriber’s DEA number.', r:'Background', why:'The Background gives the patient, the prescription and the clinical facts (Week 11). Acetaminophen/codeine is CS-III in the Basic Drug Knowledge list, and Intro to Dispensing slide 18 requires the DEA number on CIII–V prescriptions. These are facts, and the missing number is the conclusion.'}],
 teach:'Controlled prescriptions for schedules III–V need the prescriber’s DEA number even when the other information is in the pharmacy record (Intro to Dispensing slides 18–19). A missing DEA number is a prescribing error, and the EOOR key corrects it by contacting the prescriber for the DEA number (stations 6 and 13). In the SBAR the schedule and the requirement are Background facts, the missing number is the Assessment, and the request for the number is the Recommendation. Note that the key asks for the number from the prescriber; it does not have the pharmacist look it up and add it.',
 cite:'Intro-to-Dispensing.pdf slides 18–19, 38; EOOR-Answer-Key.pdf stations 6 and 13; 2025-2026 Basic Drug Knowledge List.pdf (CS-III)'},

{id:'sb05', skill:'apply', type:'match', prof:'Dr. Lahoud', tier:'new', topic:'sbarl', sub:'sort', concept:'sbar-sort-levothyroxine-strength',
 tags:['SBAR','prescribing error','strength','levothyroxine'], source:'slide',
 stem:'A pharmacist calls about a prescription written as levothyroxine 25 mg tablets, one tablet daily. Match each sentence of the call to the SBAR part it belongs to.',
 left:'Sentence from the call', right:'SBAR part',
 pairs:[
  {l:'The patient is a 60-year-old adult, the prescription reads levothyroxine 25 mg, and levothyroxine is dosed in micrograms, so 25 mg equals 25,000 mcg.', r:'Background', why:'The Background gives the patient, the prescription and the clinical facts (Week 11). That levothyroxine is dosed in micrograms is the fact that the Week 11 example of an incorrect strength relies on (25 mg instead of 25 mcg), and 25 mg = 25,000 mcg is arithmetic, not yet a conclusion.'},
  {l:'I recommend a new prescription for levothyroxine 25 mcg, one tablet daily, if that is the strength you intended.', r:'Recommendation', why:'The Recommendation says what should be done to correct the problem (Week 11). The sentence asks the prescriber for a corrected prescription and names the corrected strength while leaving the decision with the prescriber. A prescribing error is corrected by contacting the prescriber (Intro to Dispensing slide 39).'},
  {l:'The strength prescribed is incorrect: 25 mg is 1,000 times 25 mcg, and a dose that large could harm the patient.', r:'Assessment', why:'This sentence names the actual problem, an incorrect strength, and why it matters, which is the risk to the patient (Week 11). It is a conclusion drawn from the Background facts. Week 11 lists incorrect strength prescribed, for example levothyroxine 25 mg instead of 25 mcg, as a prescribing error.'},
  {l:'I am calling about a levothyroxine prescription you sent to our pharmacy today.', r:'Situation', why:'The Situation is the reason for the call (Week 11). The sentence says which prescription prompted the call and nothing more. Adding the unit problem here would skip the Background, and the listener would hear a conclusion before any of the facts that support it.'}],
 teach:'Incorrect strength prescribed is a prescribing error; the Week 11 example is levothyroxine 25 mg instead of 25 mcg. Because 1 mg = 1,000 mcg, 25 mg is 25,000 mcg, which is 1,000 times the intended 25 mcg. In the SBAR the unit fact and the arithmetic are Background, the statement that the strength is wrong and could harm the patient is the Assessment, and the Recommendation asks the prescriber for a corrected prescription. The pharmacist does not rewrite the strength alone, because a prescribing error is corrected by contacting the prescriber (slide 39).',
 cite:'APC-III-Week-11-OSCE-Prep_2026.pdf (types of prescribing errors); Intro-to-Dispensing.pdf slides 38–39'},

/* ---- Lahoud: spot the flaw in a written SBAR ---------------------------- */
{id:'sb06', skill:'error', prof:'Dr. Lahoud', tier:'new', topic:'sbarl', sub:'flaw', concept:'sbar-flaw-assessment-fact',
 tags:['SBAR','flaw','Assessment'], source:'slide',
 stem:'A pharmacist leaves this SBAR for a prescriber. S: I am calling about a Focalin XR prescription for your patient. B: The patient is a 24-year-old adult; the prescription is Focalin XR 10 mg, one capsule twice daily, and Focalin XR is an extended-release product given once daily in the morning. A: Focalin XR is an extended-release product. R: Please send a new prescription for Focalin XR 10 mg, one capsule once daily in the morning. Which flaw does this SBAR contain?',
 options:[
  {t:'The Assessment repeats a clinical fact and does not state the problem or why it matters', correct:true, why:'Week 11: the Assessment is the actual problem and why it matters, and a clinical fact belongs in the Background. Here the Assessment only restates that Focalin XR is extended-release. It never says that twice-daily dosing makes the frequency prescribed too high, which is the problem the listener needs to hear.'},
  {t:'The Recommendation is too vague to tell the prescriber what should be corrected on the prescription', correct:false, why:'The Recommendation names the drug, the strength and the exact directions to write (one capsule once daily in the morning), so the prescriber knows what to change. Week 11 asks for a recommendation that corrects the problem, and this one does. The weak part of the call is the Assessment.'},
  {t:'The Background does not identify who the patient is or which drug was prescribed', correct:false, why:'The Background gives the patient’s age and the full prescription (Focalin XR 10 mg, one capsule twice daily), which are the elements Week 11 asks for under who the patient is and what the prescription in question is. This part is complete, so the flaw lies in a different part.'},
  {t:'The Situation gives the patient’s details but does not state the reason for the call', correct:false, why:'The Situation says the call is about a Focalin XR prescription, which is the reason for the call in Week 11 terms. It does not contain patient details; the age sits in the Background where it belongs. Describing it this way misreads the one-line Situation as a flaw.'}],
 teach:'Check each part against its guiding question (Week 11). Situation: what is the reason for the call? Background: who is the patient, what is the prescription, and what are the clinical facts? Assessment: based on the Background, what is the actual problem and why does it matter? Recommendation: what should be done? A fact such as “Focalin XR is extended-release” answers the Background question. The Assessment must go further and state that an extended-release product prescribed twice daily has a frequency that is too high, which is the Week 11 error of an incorrect frequency for the formulation.',
 cite:'APC-III-Week-11-OSCE-Prep_2026.pdf (SBAR review: components; Background vs Assessment; types of prescribing errors); Intro-to-Dispensing.pdf slide 23'},

{id:'sb07', skill:'error', prof:'Dr. Lahoud', tier:'new', topic:'sbarl', sub:'flaw', concept:'sbar-flaw-vague-recommendation',
 tags:['SBAR','flaw','Recommendation','expired prescription'], source:'slide',
 stem:'A pharmacist leaves this SBAR for a prescriber. S: I am calling about a clonazepam prescription for your patient. B: The patient is 46 years old; the prescription for clonazepam 0.5 mg was written on 4/6/2026, and a CIII–V prescription expires 6 months after it is issued. A: The prescription expired on 10/6/2026, so it cannot be filled. R: Please advise us on how to proceed. Which flaw does this SBAR contain?',
 options:[
  {t:'The Recommendation does not state what the prescriber should do to correct the problem', correct:true, why:'Week 11: the Recommendation is what should be done to correct the problem. “Please advise” leaves the prescriber to guess. The EOOR key, station 6, gives the specific correction for an expired CIII–V prescription, which is to contact the prescriber for a new prescription, so the call should ask for that.'},
  {t:'The Assessment places a clinical fact where the problem should be stated, which hides the problem', correct:false, why:'The Assessment says that the prescription expired on 10/6/2026 and cannot be filled, which is the problem and its consequence. The 6-month rule is stated in the Background, where facts belong (Week 11). Describing the Assessment as a fact misreads a complete conclusion as a flaw.'},
  {t:'The Background leaves out the date that the prescription was written', correct:false, why:'The Background gives 4/6/2026 as the date written and the 6-month expiration rule for CIII–V prescriptions (Intro to Dispensing slide 22). Those are the facts needed to understand the problem, so nothing is missing. The flaw is in the last part of the call.'},
  {t:'The Situation lists the expiration date instead of the reason for the call', correct:false, why:'The Situation says only that the call concerns a clonazepam prescription, which is a short reason for the call (Week 11). The dates sit in the Background and the Assessment. A reader who sees dates in the Situation has attached them to the wrong sentence.'}],
 teach:'A prescription expires 6 months after issue for schedules III–V (Intro to Dispensing slide 22; EOOR key, station 6). 4/6/2026 plus 6 months is 10/6/2026, and as of 11/21/2026 that date passed 46 days ago (25 days left in October plus 21 in November). An expired prescription is a prescribing error, so the pharmacist calls the prescriber and asks for a new prescription. “Please advise” is not a Recommendation: it does not say what should be done. A good Recommendation can be answered with a yes or a no.',
 cite:'Intro-to-Dispensing.pdf slides 22, 38–39; EOOR-Answer-Key.pdf station 6; APC-III-Week-11-OSCE-Prep_2026.pdf'},

{id:'sb08', skill:'error', prof:'Dr. Lahoud', tier:'new', topic:'sbarl', sub:'flaw', concept:'sbar-flaw-no-patient-drug',
 tags:['SBAR','flaw','Background'], source:'slide',
 stem:'A pharmacist leaves this SBAR for a prescriber. S: I am calling about a prescription you sent today. B: It was written with 2 refills, and schedule II prescriptions are allowed no refills. A: The number of refills prescribed is inappropriate for a schedule II drug. R: I recommend a new prescription with no refills. Which flaw does this SBAR contain?',
 options:[
  {t:'The Background never identifies the patient or the drug that was prescribed', correct:true, why:'Week 11: the Background answers who the patient is and what the prescription in question is. This call never names the patient or the drug, so the prescriber cannot tell which prescription is meant. Every other part reads well, but it cannot be acted on without that identification.'},
  {t:'The Recommendation has the pharmacist change the refills instead of asking the prescriber', correct:false, why:'The Recommendation asks for a new prescription with no refills, which leaves the correction with the prescriber. Intro to Dispensing slide 39 corrects a prescribing error by contacting the prescriber to obtain a new prescription, so this wording follows the rule and is not the flaw.'},
  {t:'The Assessment gives the rule again and never states the problem with this prescription', correct:false, why:'The Assessment states that the number of refills prescribed is inappropriate for a schedule II drug, which is the problem in the Week 11 list of prescribing errors (inappropriate number of refills, for example refills for CII). The rule itself is in the Background, so the parts are separate.'},
  {t:'The Situation is missing, because the call begins with a prescription and not a reason', correct:false, why:'The first sentence says that the call is about a prescription sent today, which is a reason for the call in Week 11 terms. It is short by design. The missing piece in this SBAR is who the patient is and which drug was written, and that belongs in the Background.'}],
 teach:'The Background must let the listener find the prescription: who the patient is, what drug and directions were written, and the clinical facts that matter (Week 11). A call that says “a prescription you sent today” without a patient or a drug fails that step even when the rule and the correction are right. Prescribers send many prescriptions in a day. Name the patient, the drug and the strength early, then state the fact, then the problem. The correction here, a new prescription without refills, matches the EOOR key for refills on a CII (station 13).',
 cite:'APC-III-Week-11-OSCE-Prep_2026.pdf (SBAR review: components); Intro-to-Dispensing.pdf slides 22, 39; EOOR-Answer-Key.pdf station 13'},

{id:'sb09', skill:'error', prof:'Dr. Lahoud', tier:'new', topic:'sbarl', sub:'flaw', concept:'sbar-flaw-order',
 tags:['SBAR','flaw','logical order'], source:'slide',
 stem:'A pharmacist reads this SBAR aloud in the order written. S: I am calling about a sulfamethoxazole/trimethoprim suspension prescription for your patient. R: I recommend a new prescription for 75 mL. B: The patient is 31 years old, the prescription is sulfamethoxazole/trimethoprim suspension 7.5 mL twice daily for 5 days, which needs 75 mL, and 60 mL was prescribed. A: The quantity prescribed is insufficient, and the patient would run out after 4 days. Which flaw does this SBAR contain?',
 options:[
  {t:'The Recommendation is spoken before the Background and Assessment, out of logical order', correct:true, why:'Week 11 tells students to communicate the SBAR in logical order: Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation. Slide 20 of the immunization deck says to think in reverse when formulating an SBAR, which is a drafting tip, not the order of delivery. Here the request comes before the facts that justify it.'},
  {t:'The Assessment is missing, so the listener does not hear the problem with the prescription', correct:false, why:'The last sentence is an Assessment: it states that the quantity prescribed is insufficient and that the patient would run out after 4 days (60 mL divided by 15 mL per day). The problem and its consequence are present, but they come last instead of third.'},
  {t:'The Background leaves out the quantity that was prescribed, so the problem is hard to judge', correct:false, why:'The Background states that 60 mL was prescribed and that the directions need 75 mL (7.5 mL × 2 × 5 days). Those are the facts needed to judge the quantity. Week 11 asks for the patient, the prescription and the clinical facts, and all three appear in that sentence.'},
  {t:'The Situation does not give a reason for the call, so the prescriber does not know why the call was made', correct:false, why:'The Situation says that the call concerns a sulfamethoxazole/trimethoprim suspension prescription for the prescriber’s patient, which is the reason for the call in Week 11 terms. The listener knows what is being discussed, and the order of the later parts is where the call goes wrong.'}],
 teach:'Week 11 tips: write an SBAR outline before the station, and communicate the SBAR in logical order. The immunization deck (slide 20) adds that when a student has trouble formulating an SBAR, working in reverse from the recommendation helps. That is a way to draft, not a way to speak: the call is delivered as Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation, so the prescriber hears why you are calling, the facts, the problem and then the request. Be complete but concise (slide 20).',
 cite:'APC-III-Week-11-OSCE-Prep_2026.pdf (SBAR for EOOR: tips); Immunizations-Pre-Lab.pdf slide 20'},

{id:'sb10', skill:'error', prof:'Dr. Lahoud', tier:'new', topic:'sbarl', sub:'flaw', concept:'sbar-flaw-pharmacist-changes-dose',
 tags:['SBAR','flaw','Recommendation','levothyroxine'], source:'slide',
 stem:'A pharmacist leaves this SBAR for a prescriber. S: I am calling about a levothyroxine prescription you sent today. B: The patient is a 60-year-old adult; the prescription reads levothyroxine 25 mg, one tablet daily, and levothyroxine is dosed in micrograms. A: The strength prescribed is incorrect: 25 mg is 1,000 times 25 mcg, and a dose that large could harm the patient. R: I will change the strength to 25 mcg on our label and fill it today. Which flaw does this SBAR contain?',
 options:[
  {t:'The Recommendation has the pharmacist correct the prescription alone instead of asking the prescriber', correct:true, why:'A prescribing error is corrected by contacting the prescriber to clarify, correct or obtain a new prescription (Intro to Dispensing slide 39). Changing the strength on the label is the fix for a transcribing error, not for a strength the prescriber wrote. The pharmacist recommends the correction, and the prescriber issues it.'},
  {t:'The Assessment does not explain why the incorrect strength matters to the patient', correct:false, why:'The Assessment states that 25 mg is 1,000 times 25 mcg and that a dose that large could harm the patient, which is the why-it-matters element that Week 11 asks for. The problem and its consequence are both present, so the Assessment is not the flawed part of this call.'},
  {t:'The Background states the problem instead of the clinical facts about the prescription, which belongs in the Assessment', correct:false, why:'The Background gives the patient, the prescription as written and the fact that levothyroxine is dosed in micrograms. None of these draws a conclusion. Week 11 keeps the fact in the Background and the problem in the Assessment, and this call follows that split correctly.'},
  {t:'The Situation names the prescriber but not the drug that is the subject of the call', correct:false, why:'The Situation says that the call concerns a levothyroxine prescription sent today, so the drug is named. Week 11 asks the Situation to give the reason for the call in a short line, which this one does. The listener never has to guess which drug is meant.'}],
 teach:'Match the fix to the error type (Intro to Dispensing slides 38–39). Prescribing errors are corrected by contacting the prescriber to clarify, correct or obtain a new prescription. Transcribing errors are corrected by re-entering the prescription and regenerating the label, and dispensing errors by replacing the product or relabeling. A strength that the prescriber wrote wrongly is a prescribing error, so a pharmacist who changes the strength alone has acted outside the correction. The Recommendation in an SBAR is a request to the prescriber, such as a new prescription for levothyroxine 25 mcg if that was the intent.',
 cite:'Intro-to-Dispensing.pdf slides 38–39; EOOR-Answer-Key.pdf station 7 (contact prescriber with new recommendation); APC-III-Week-11-OSCE-Prep_2026.pdf'},

{id:'sb11', skill:'apply', prof:'Dr. Lahoud', multi:true, tier:'new', topic:'sbarl', sub:'flaw', concept:'sbar-background-ibuprofen-multi',
 tags:['SBAR','Background','Assessment'], source:'slide',
 stem:'A pharmacist writes an SBAR about an ibuprofen prescription for 800 mg every 4 hours as needed, written for a 46-year-old patient. Which statements belong in the Background? Select all that apply.',
 options:[
  {t:'The patient is 46 years old and was prescribed ibuprofen 800 mg every 4 hours as needed', correct:true, why:'Week 11 puts who the patient is and what the prescription in question is in the Background. Age and the written directions identify the case and draw no conclusion. They are facts that the listener needs before the problem can be understood, so they belong in this part of the call.'},
  {t:'The maximum dose of ibuprofen is 3.2 g/day', correct:true, why:'This is the Week 11 example of a Background statement: the maximum prescribed dose of ibuprofen is 3.2 g/day. It is a clinical fact needed to understand the situation. It does not yet say that the prescription is wrong, so it is not an Assessment.'},
  {t:'The total daily dose prescribed is too high', correct:false, why:'This is the Week 11 example of an Assessment. It states the actual problem that follows from comparing the written directions with the maximum. Placing it in the Background would announce the conclusion before the facts that support it have been given.'},
  {t:'I recommend a new prescription at 800 mg every 8 hours as needed', correct:false, why:'This sentence says what should be done to correct the problem, which is the Recommendation (Week 11). Even though it contains the same drug and strength as the Background, its job in the call is the request to the prescriber.'},
  {t:'I am calling about the ibuprofen prescription you sent today', correct:false, why:'This sentence is the reason for the call, which Week 11 assigns to the Situation. It repeats the drug name but contains no patient, no directions and no fact, so it does not give the listener what the Background gives.'}],
 teach:'Background answers three questions: who is the patient, what is the prescription in question, and what are the clinical facts surrounding the problem (Week 11). Here that is the age, the written directions and the maximum of 3.2 g/day. Assessment answers a different question: based on those facts, what is the actual problem and why does it matter? The total daily dose prescribed is too high. When two sentences use the same numbers, ask which one reports a fact and which one judges the prescription. Only the judgment is the Assessment.',
 cite:'APC-III-Week-11-OSCE-Prep_2026.pdf (SBAR review: components; Background vs Assessment, example #1)'},

{id:'sb12', skill:'apply', prof:'Dr. Lahoud', multi:true, tier:'new', topic:'sbarl', sub:'flaw', concept:'sbar-background-expired-multi',
 tags:['SBAR','Background','expired prescription','date arithmetic'], source:'slide',
 stem:'On 11/21/2026 a pharmacist writes an SBAR about a clonazepam 0.5 mg prescription for a 46-year-old patient. It was written on 4/6/2026. Which statements belong in the Background? Select all that apply.',
 options:[
  {t:'The patient is 46 years old, and the clonazepam 0.5 mg prescription was written on 4/6/2026', correct:true, why:'Week 11 puts who the patient is and the prescription in question in the Background. The date written is the fact that the expiration rule is applied to, and it draws no conclusion. The call cannot reach the problem without giving it first.'},
  {t:'A CIII–V prescription expires 6 months after the date of issuance', correct:true, why:'Intro to Dispensing slide 22 gives 6 months for schedule III–V, and the EOOR key, station 6, uses the same rule. It is a clinical and regulatory fact needed to understand the situation, so it belongs in the Background. Applying it to this prescription is the next step.'},
  {t:'The prescription expired on 10/6/2026, which was 46 days before today', correct:false, why:'4/6/2026 plus 6 months is 10/6/2026, and 25 days left in October plus 21 in November is 46 days before 11/21/2026. This applies the rule to the case and states the problem, so it is the Assessment (Week 11), not a background fact.'},
  {t:'I recommend that you send a new prescription for clonazepam', correct:false, why:'This sentence says what should be done to correct the problem, which Week 11 assigns to the Recommendation. The EOOR key, station 6, names this correction for an expired CIII–V prescription: contact the prescriber for a new prescription.'},
  {t:'I am calling about an expired prescription for your patient', correct:false, why:'This sentence gives the reason for the call, which is the Situation in Week 11. It also states a conclusion, expired, before the dates have been given, which is why it does not fit the Background even though it is about the same prescription.'}],
 teach:'Prescription expiration: dangerous drugs 1 year, schedule III–V 6 months, schedule II 30 days (Intro to Dispensing slide 22; the EOOR key words the CII limit as more than 1 month). For this case, 4/6/2026 plus 6 months is 10/6/2026, which is 46 days before 11/21/2026. The rule and the date written are Background facts. The conclusion that the prescription expired is the Assessment, and the request for a new prescription is the Recommendation. An expired prescription is a prescribing error, so the pharmacist calls the prescriber (EOOR key, station 6).',
 cite:'Intro-to-Dispensing.pdf slide 22; EOOR-Answer-Key.pdf station 6; APC-III-Week-11-OSCE-Prep_2026.pdf'},

/* ---- Lahoud: case reasoning -------------------------------------------- */
{id:'sb13', skill:'apply', prof:'Dr. Lahoud', tier:'new', topic:'sbarl', sub:'case', concept:'sbar-case-pick-levothyroxine',
 tags:['SBAR','case','strength','levothyroxine'], source:'slide',
 stem:'A prescription arrives for levothyroxine 25 mg tablets, 30 tablets, one tablet daily, 2 refills, for a 60-year-old patient. The patient’s record, the label and the bottle all match the prescription. Which is the correct and complete SBAR to the prescriber?',
 options:[
  {t:'S: I am calling about the levothyroxine prescription you sent today. B: The patient is 60 years old, the prescription is levothyroxine 25 mg once daily, and levothyroxine is dosed in micrograms. A: The strength is incorrect, since 25 mg is 1,000 times 25 mcg, which could harm the patient. R: I recommend a new prescription for 25 mcg if that is the strength you intended.', correct:true, why:'It identifies the error Week 11 lists, an incorrect strength (levothyroxine 25 mg instead of 25 mcg), puts the unit fact in the Background and the conclusion in the Assessment with why it matters, and asks the prescriber for a corrected prescription (Intro to Dispensing slide 39).'},
  {t:'S: I am calling about the levothyroxine prescription you sent today. B: The patient is 60 years old, and the prescription is levothyroxine 25 mg once daily with 2 refills. A: The quantity is insufficient, since 30 tablets will not last the patient, which could harm care. R: I recommend a new prescription for a larger quantity of levothyroxine tablets so that treatment is not interrupted.', correct:false, why:'It names the wrong error. Nothing in the case makes 30 tablets for a once-daily drug insufficient, and the Week 11 quantity error is a liquid problem. The real error is the unit of the strength, so this call sends the prescriber to fix something that is not wrong.'},
  {t:'S: I am calling about the levothyroxine prescription you sent today. B: The patient is 60 years old, and the prescription is levothyroxine 25 mg once daily. A: The strength written on the prescription is levothyroxine 25 mg. R: Please look at the strength on this prescription and let me know what you want.', correct:false, why:'The Assessment repeats a fact instead of stating the problem and why it matters (Week 11), and the Recommendation is vague. The prescriber hears the strength read back and a request to look, but never hears that the unit is wrong or what to write instead.'},
  {t:'S: I am calling about the levothyroxine prescription you sent today. B: The patient is 60 years old, and the prescription is levothyroxine 25 mg once daily, dosed in micrograms. A: The strength is incorrect, since 25 mg is 1,000 times 25 mcg, which could harm the patient. R: I will change the strength to 25 mcg and fill it as soon as I finish this call.', correct:false, why:'The error is identified correctly, but the Recommendation has the pharmacist change a strength that the prescriber wrote. A prescribing error is corrected by contacting the prescriber to clarify, correct or obtain a new prescription (Intro to Dispensing slide 39).'}],
 teach:'Three checks separate a complete SBAR from a near miss. First, is the error identified correctly (strength, not quantity or refills)? Second, does the Background hold the facts (patient, prescription, units) while the Assessment states the problem and why it matters? Third, does the Recommendation ask the prescriber for a specific correction instead of leaving the decision open or making the change alone? Week 11 lists incorrect strength prescribed, for example levothyroxine 25 mg instead of 25 mcg, as a prescribing error, and prescribing errors are the ones that go to the prescriber.',
 cite:'APC-III-Week-11-OSCE-Prep_2026.pdf (types of prescribing errors; SBAR components); Intro-to-Dispensing.pdf slides 38–39'},

{id:'sb14', skill:'error', prof:'Dr. Lahoud', tier:'new', topic:'sbarl', sub:'case', concept:'sbar-case-which-needs-call',
 tags:['SBAR','error type','who fixes it','EOOR'], source:'slide',
 stem:'During verification on 11/21/2026, a pharmacist finds one problem on each of four prescriptions. Which problem calls for an SBAR to the prescriber?',
 options:[
  {t:'A clonazepam prescription written on 4/6/2026 is presented for filling', correct:true, why:'Schedule III–V prescriptions expire 6 months after issue (Intro to Dispensing slide 22), so this one expired on 10/6/2026. An expired prescription is a prescribing error, and the correction is to contact the prescriber for a new prescription (EOOR key, station 6; slide 39).'},
  {t:'The prescription reads once daily, but the label printed by the pharmacy system says twice daily', correct:false, why:'The prescription is correct and the label is not, so this is a transcribing error (directions transcribed incorrectly onto the label; EOOR key, station 2). The correction is to work with the technician to fix the label, with no SBAR to the prescriber.'},
  {t:'The bottle on the counter holds apixaban, but the prescription is for Xarelto (rivaroxaban)', correct:false, why:'The prescription is correct, and the wrong drug was filled, so this is a dispensing error (incorrect drug filled; EOOR key, station 8). The pharmacist works with the technician to get the right stock bottle and reverify. The prescriber has made no error to correct.'},
  {t:'The prescription is for 75 mL, but 60 mL was dispensed and labeled for the patient', correct:false, why:'The prescriber wrote 75 mL, so the shortfall happened at filling: insufficient quantity filled is a dispensing error (EOOR key, station 9). The pharmacy fills the full amount and reverifies. Only the prescribing errors on the Week 11 list require a call to the prescriber.'}],
 teach:'Sort the problem by where it entered the process (Intro to Dispensing slides 38–39). If the prescriber wrote it wrongly, it is a prescribing error and is corrected by contacting the prescriber. If the prescription was entered or labeled wrongly, it is a transcribing error, corrected by re-entering and regenerating the label. If the wrong product, strength, formulation or quantity was filled, it is a dispensing error, corrected by replacing the product or relabeling. Week 11 asks which error requires an SBAR to the provider, and the answer is the prescribing error.',
 cite:'Intro-to-Dispensing.pdf slides 22, 38–39; EOOR-Answer-Key.pdf stations 2, 6, 8 and 9; APC-III-Week-11-OSCE-Prep_2026.pdf'},

{id:'sb15', skill:'error', prof:'Dr. Lahoud', tier:'new', topic:'sbarl', sub:'case', concept:'sbar-case-quantity-prescribed-vs-filled',
 tags:['SBAR','error type','quantity','liquid'], source:'slide',
 stem:'A prescription reads sulfamethoxazole/trimethoprim suspension, 7.5 mL twice daily for 5 days, quantity 60 mL. The technician entered 60 mL, and the filled bottle and its label also show 60 mL. What is the error, and who corrects it?',
 options:[
  {t:'Prescribing error: insufficient quantity prescribed, so the pharmacist contacts the prescriber', correct:true, why:'The directions need 75 mL (7.5 mL × 2 × 5 days), but the prescriber wrote 60 mL. Insufficient prescribed quantity is on the Week 11 list of prescribing errors and is common with liquids. A prescribing error is corrected by contacting the prescriber for a new prescription (slide 39).'},
  {t:'Dispensing error: insufficient quantity filled, so the pharmacist works with the technician to fill 75 mL', correct:false, why:'EOOR station 9 is a dispensing error because less was filled than the prescription called for. Here the pharmacy filled exactly the 60 mL that was written. Filling 75 mL without a new prescription would change what the prescriber ordered, so the technician cannot correct this.'},
  {t:'Transcribing error: quantity entered incorrectly, so the technician re-enters it and regenerates the label', correct:false, why:'A transcribing error means the entry differs from the prescription (EOOR key, stations 2 and 4). Here the entry and the label both match the 60 mL written, so there was nothing to re-enter. The shortfall began on the prescription, which the technician cannot change.'},
  {t:'No error: the quantity on the prescription is the quantity to fill, and counseling covers the rest', correct:false, why:'Verification step 1 asks whether the prescription is legal and safe, including whether the quantity is within reason (Intro to Dispensing slide 32). 60 mL lasts 4 days at 15 mL a day, so the patient would run out before the 5-day course ends.'}],
 teach:'The same 60 mL versus 75 mL numbers can be a prescribing error or a dispensing error, and the owner of the fix differs. Prescribing: the prescription itself is short, so the pharmacist calls the prescriber (Week 11 list: insufficient prescribed quantity, common with liquids). Dispensing: the prescription is right and less was filled (EOOR key, station 9), so the pharmacist works with the technician. Compare each downstream step with the prescription first. Here the label and bottle match the prescription, so the problem is upstream of the pharmacy.',
 cite:'APC-III-Week-11-OSCE-Prep_2026.pdf (types of prescribing errors); Intro-to-Dispensing.pdf slides 32, 38–39; EOOR-Answer-Key.pdf stations 2, 4 and 9'},

{id:'sb16', skill:'apply', prof:'Dr. Lahoud', tier:'new', topic:'sbarl', sub:'case', concept:'sbar-case-recommendation-cii',
 tags:['SBAR','Recommendation','refills','CII'], source:'slide',
 stem:'A Focalin XR 10 mg prescription for 30 capsules, one capsule every morning, was written with 2 refills. Everything else on it is complete and correct. The pharmacist’s Background and Assessment state that schedule II prescriptions are allowed no refills and that the 2 refills are inappropriate. Which Recommendation finishes the SBAR?',
 options:[
  {t:'I recommend a new prescription for Focalin XR 10 mg, 30 capsules, with no refills.', correct:true, why:'It states a specific correction that matches the problem, and it leaves the decision with the prescriber. Slide 22 gives 0 refills for schedule II, and the EOOR key, station 13, corrects refills on a CII by calling the provider. The prescriber can answer with a yes.'},
  {t:'I will remove the refills in our system and fill the prescription for 30 capsules today.', correct:false, why:'This has the pharmacist alter what the prescriber wrote. Refills on the prescription are a prescribing error, and it is corrected by contacting the prescriber, not by editing the entry (Intro to Dispensing slide 39). Editing the system would also leave the paper or electronic prescription unchanged.'},
  {t:'I recommend a new prescription for Focalin XR 10 mg with up to 5 refills in 6 months.', correct:false, why:'Up to 5 refills in 6 months is the limit for schedule III–V prescriptions (slide 27). Schedule II allows no refills, so this recommendation repeats the same error with a different number. It also fails to match the Assessment the pharmacist just gave.'},
  {t:'Please review the prescription and let us know whether changes are needed in the directions.', correct:false, why:'This is vague. It does not say what should be done to correct the problem (Week 11 Recommendation), and it points to the directions, which are not the issue. The prescriber would have to find the problem again before answering anything useful.'}],
 teach:'A good Recommendation does three things: it matches the problem named in the Assessment, it is specific enough to answer yes or no, and it leaves a prescribing error to the prescriber (Week 11; Intro to Dispensing slide 39). Refill limits by category are 0 for schedule II, up to 5 in 6 months for schedule III–V, and no limit for dangerous drugs (slides 22 and 27). Recommending a number of refills that fits the wrong schedule is a near miss that sounds specific but repeats the error.',
 cite:'Intro-to-Dispensing.pdf slides 22, 27, 38–39; EOOR-Answer-Key.pdf station 13; APC-III-Week-11-OSCE-Prep_2026.pdf'},

/* ---- Parsons: vaccine-needs SBAR --------------------------------------- */
{id:'sb17', skill:'apply', type:'match', prof:'Dr. Parsons', tier:'new', topic:'sbarp', sub:'sort', concept:'sbar-vaccine-sort-pregnancy', img:'aafp_t2',
 tags:['SBAR','vaccine-needs SBAR','pregnancy','AAFP Table 2'], source:'both',
 stem:'On 11/21/2026 a pharmacy intern writes a vaccine-needs SBAR for the preceptor about a pregnant patient. Match each sentence to the SBAR part it belongs to.',
 left:'Sentence from the SBAR', right:'SBAR part',
 pairs:[
  {l:'AAFP Table 2 recommends inactivated influenza, COVID-19 and Tdap in pregnancy, and Abrysvo at 32 weeks 0 days through 36 weeks 6 days from September through January; LAIV3, MMR and varicella are contraindicated.', r:'Assessment', why:'In the vaccine-needs SBAR (Immunizations slide 22) the Assessment states what the AAFP schedule recommends for the patient, and (T) the source is cited. Table 2 shows yellow for influenza and COVID-19, orange for Tdap and red for LAIV3, MMR and varicella in pregnancy; the Notes give the Abrysvo window.'},
  {l:'A patient is at the pharmacy to be assessed for vaccine needs.', r:'Situation', why:'Slide 22 opens the example with a simple reason, that the patient is at the pharmacy to be assessed for vaccine needs, and (T) the situation should be something pretty simple, such as the patient coming to the pharmacy. It carries no patient characteristic and no recommendation.'},
  {l:'I recommend an inactivated influenza vaccine, a COVID-19 vaccine, Tdap and Abrysvo today.', r:'Recommendation', why:'Slide 22 closes with “I recommend” followed by the vaccines, and the Recommendation says what should be done. At 33 weeks on 11/21/2026 the patient is inside the Tdap range (weeks 27–36) and the Abrysvo window (32 weeks 0 days to 36 weeks 6 days, September through January).'},
  {l:'She is 28 years old and 33 weeks pregnant today, has received no vaccines this pregnancy, and her screening checklist was negative.', r:'Background', why:'Slide 22 puts patient characteristics and vaccine history in the Background, and (T) the background is the characteristics that back up or are required for the assessment. Pregnancy and gestational age are the facts that the Table 2 column and the Notes depend on.'}],
 teach:'This is the vaccine-needs SBAR (Immunizations slide 22), which differs from the prescription-error SBAR. Background holds patient characteristics and vaccine history; Assessment states what the AAFP schedule recommends for that patient, with the source cited (T); Recommendation lists the vaccines to give. In the pregnancy column of Table 2, yellow means recommended, orange means recommended with additional considerations (Tdap each pregnancy), and red means contraindicated or not recommended (LAIV3, MMR, varicella, HPV). (T) If it is hard to write, work backward from the recommendation.',
 cite:'Immunizations-Pre-Lab.pdf slides 20–22; 9/21 transcript (T); AAFP-Adult-Schedule-2026.pdf Table 2 and Notes (RSV, Tdap)'},

{id:'sb18', skill:'apply', type:'match', prof:'Dr. Parsons', tier:'new', topic:'sbarp', sub:'sort', concept:'sbar-vaccine-sort-hiv-mmr', img:'aafp_t2',
 tags:['SBAR','vaccine-needs SBAR','HIV','CD4','MMR'], source:'both',
 stem:'A pharmacy intern writes a vaccine-needs SBAR for the preceptor about a 35-year-old patient with HIV who asks for an MMR vaccine. Match each sentence to the SBAR part it belongs to.',
 left:'Sentence from the SBAR', right:'SBAR part',
 pairs:[
  {l:'I recommend that we not give MMR today and recheck his CD4 count; if it reaches at least 200 cells/mm3 and 15% for at least 6 months, he can receive 2 doses at least 4 weeks apart.', r:'Recommendation', why:'The Recommendation says what to do (slide 22). Here it is to withhold MMR now and to plan for a later 2-dose series, which follows the Notes: 2 doses at least 4 weeks apart when CD4 is at least 15% and at least 200 cells/mm3 for at least 6 months and there is no evidence of immunity.'},
  {l:'AAFP Table 2 and the Notes state that MMR is contraindicated in HIV when the CD4 count is below 200 cells/mm3 or the CD4 percentage is below 15%, so he should not receive it today.', r:'Assessment', why:'In the vaccine-needs SBAR the Assessment states the AAFP source and what it says about this patient (slide 22; (T) cite the source). The schedule’s rule is applied to his CD4 count here, which is the interpretation step that follows the background facts.'},
  {l:'A patient is at the pharmacy asking for an MMR vaccine.', r:'Situation', why:'The Situation is a simple reason for the contact (slide 22; (T)). This sentence says why the patient is at the pharmacy and nothing more. His HIV status and CD4 count are the characteristics that support the assessment, so they are placed in the Background.'},
  {l:'He is 35 years old, has HIV with a CD4 count of 180 cells/mm3 today, has no documented MMR doses, and his screening checklist was negative.', r:'Background', why:'Slide 22 puts patient characteristics and vaccine history in the Background, and (T) these are the characteristics that back up the assessment. The CD4 count of 180 cells/mm3 is a patient fact. What the AAFP schedule says about that count is the next sentence.'}],
 teach:'In the vaccine-needs SBAR the line between Background and Assessment is different from the prescription-error SBAR. Background holds what is true about the patient: age, HIV, the CD4 count and the vaccine history. Assessment holds what the schedule says about that patient: MMR is contraindicated when the CD4 percentage is below 15% or the count is below 200 cells/mm3 (Table 2: red; Notes). The Recommendation turns that into an action. The count of 180 is a fact to report, and the rule is what interprets it.',
 cite:'Immunizations-Pre-Lab.pdf slides 20–22; 9/21 transcript (T); AAFP-Adult-Schedule-2026.pdf Table 2 and Notes (MMR, HIV)'},

{id:'sb19', skill:'apply', prof:'Dr. Parsons', tier:'new', topic:'sbarp', sub:'rec', concept:'sbar-vaccine-assessment-30-weeks', img:'aafp_t2',
 tags:['SBAR','vaccine-needs SBAR','pregnancy','RSV','Tdap','date arithmetic'], source:'both',
 stem:'On 11/21/2026 a patient who is 30 weeks 0 days pregnant has received no vaccines this pregnancy and has a negative screening checklist. Which Assessment is correct for the SBAR to the preceptor?',
 options:[
  {t:'AAFP recommends Tdap, inactivated influenza and COVID-19 vaccine now; Abrysvo is due from 32 weeks 0 days (12/5/2026)', correct:true, why:'Tdap is recommended in each pregnancy, preferably early in weeks 27–36, and Table 2 shows yellow for influenza and COVID-19. The Abrysvo window opens at 32 weeks 0 days, 14 days after 11/21, which is 12/5/2026, and the season runs September through January, so it is not yet due.'},
  {t:'AAFP recommends Abrysvo today because 11/21/2026 is inside the September–January season, with Tdap and inactivated influenza vaccine', correct:false, why:'The Notes give two conditions for Abrysvo: 32 weeks 0 days through 36 weeks 6 days, and September through January. Being in season is not enough. At 30 weeks the patient is 2 weeks short of the window, so the season alone does not make the dose due today.'},
  {t:'AAFP recommends waiting to give Tdap until 36 weeks, and giving inactivated influenza and COVID-19 vaccine now', correct:false, why:'The Notes recommend 1 dose of Tdap during each pregnancy, preferably in the early part of weeks 27–36. At 30 weeks the patient is already in that range, so waiting to 36 weeks gives up the preferred timing. The two other vaccines in this answer are correct.'},
  {t:'AAFP recommends FluMist (LAIV3) with Tdap now, and Abrysvo from 32 weeks 0 days, which is 12/5/2026, inside the season', correct:false, why:'This answer has the correct dates but the wrong influenza vaccine. LAIV3 is red in the pregnancy column of Table 2, and the Appendix lists pregnancy as a contraindication to LAIV3. An inactivated or recombinant influenza vaccine is the choice, which Table 2 shows in yellow.'}],
 teach:'For a pregnant patient, read the pregnancy column of Table 2 and then the Notes for each vaccine. Yellow (influenza inactivated or recombinant, COVID-19) is recommended; orange (Tdap) is recommended each pregnancy; red (LAIV3, MMR, varicella, HPV) is contraindicated or not recommended. Abrysvo needs both a gestational window (32 weeks 0 days through 36 weeks 6 days) and a season (September through January). Compute the date: 30 weeks 0 days plus 14 days is 32 weeks 0 days, which falls on 12/5/2026. In the Assessment, name the AAFP as the source (T).',
 cite:'AAFP-Adult-Schedule-2026.pdf Table 2, Notes (RSV, Tdap) and Appendix (LAIV3); Immunizations-Pre-Lab.pdf slide 22; 9/21 transcript (T)'},

{id:'sb20', skill:'apply', prof:'Dr. Parsons', tier:'new', topic:'sbarp', sub:'rec', concept:'sbar-vaccine-assessment-laiv3-precaution', img:'aafp_t2',
 tags:['SBAR','vaccine-needs SBAR','LAIV3','precaution','diabetes'], source:'both',
 stem:'A 38-year-old patient with type 2 diabetes asks for FluMist (LAIV3) on 11/21/2026. The patient is not pregnant, not immunocompromised and has no other medical condition. Which Assessment is correct for the SBAR to the preceptor?',
 options:[
  {t:'Diabetes is a precaution for LAIV3, not a contraindication: it may be given if the benefit outweighs the risk', correct:true, why:'In Table 2 the LAIV3 cell for diabetes is navy, which the legend defines as a precaution that might be indicated if the benefit of protection outweighs the risk. The Appendix lists metabolic disorders including diabetes under LAIV3 precautions, and the inactivated vaccines have no diabetes precaution.'},
  {t:'Diabetes is a contraindication to LAIV3, so FluMist should not be given and an inactivated influenza vaccine is recommended', correct:false, why:'Contraindicated is red in Table 2, and the Appendix footnote says that when a contraindication is present the vaccine should not be given. Diabetes is navy, a precaution, which allows a risk-benefit decision. The inactivated vaccine is a reasonable choice, but it does not make the reason a contraindication.'},
  {t:'Diabetes has no effect on LAIV3, so FluMist is recommended for this patient the same way as for a healthy adult', correct:false, why:'The cell is navy, not yellow, so something does affect it. Yellow would mean recommended for all adults lacking documentation, and navy means precaution. The Appendix lists underlying metabolic disorders, including diabetes mellitus, as a LAIV3 precaution, so the patient is not treated as a healthy adult.'},
  {t:'FluMist is recommended because the navy cell reads 1 dose annually if age 19–49 years, and a 38-year-old is in that range', correct:false, why:'The age range in the cell tells which adults the dose covers, but navy still means a precaution (Table 2 legend). The age does not remove it. A recommendation based only on the age range skips the risk-benefit decision that a precaution requires, as the Appendix footnote explains.'}],
 teach:'Read the color, then the legend. In Table 2 red means contraindicated or not recommended, so the vaccine should not be given; navy means precaution, so vaccination should generally be deferred but might be indicated if the benefit outweighs the risk (Appendix footnotes 1 and 2). LAIV3 is red for pregnancy, immunocompromised states and HIV, and navy for heart or lung disease, chronic liver disease and diabetes (the precaution does not apply to alcoholism). A precaution still leaves an inactivated vaccine as a straightforward alternative with no such precaution.',
 cite:'AAFP-Adult-Schedule-2026.pdf Table 2 (LAIV3 row; legend; footnote a) and Appendix (LAIV3 contraindications and precautions; footnotes 1–2)'},

{id:'sb21', skill:'apply', prof:'Dr. Parsons', tier:'new', topic:'sbarp', sub:'rec', concept:'sbar-vaccine-assessment-cd4-six-months', img:'aafp_t2',
 tags:['SBAR','vaccine-needs SBAR','HIV','CD4','MMR','date arithmetic'], source:'both',
 stem:'A 35-year-old patient with HIV and no documented MMR doses asks for MMR on 11/21/2026. His CD4 count and percentage first reached at least 200 cells/mm3 and 15% on 10/21/2026 and have stayed there at each check since. Which Assessment is correct for the SBAR to the preceptor?',
 options:[
  {t:'MMR is not contraindicated now, but is recommended after 6 months of stable CD4, so the earliest date is 4/21/2027', correct:true, why:'The Notes contraindicate MMR below 15% or 200 cells/mm3 and recommend 2 doses when CD4 is at least 15% and 200 for at least 6 months. 10/21/2026 plus 6 months is 4/21/2027, and the patient is 1 month into the 6, so the dose is not yet due.'},
  {t:'MMR is still contraindicated, because the CD4 count was below 200 cells/mm3 at some point in the past 6 months', correct:false, why:'The Notes make MMR contraindicated for a CD4 percentage below 15% or a count below 200 cells/mm3, which describes the current values. His values are above both thresholds now, so MMR is not contraindicated. What he is waiting for is the 6 months that the recommendation requires.'},
  {t:'MMR is recommended today as 2 doses at least 4 weeks apart, because the Table 2 cell for CD4 at least 15% and 200 is yellow', correct:false, why:'Yellow in Table 2 reads as recommended, but the Notes qualify the HIV recommendation with at least 6 months at those CD4 levels. Table 2 shows the color, and the Notes give the condition. “You cannot get the right answer if you don’t use the notes section” (T).'},
  {t:'MMR can be given after 6 months of stable values counted from today, so the earliest date is 5/21/2027', correct:false, why:'The 6 months are counted from the date the CD4 values first reached 15% and 200 cells/mm3, which was 10/21/2026, not from the date of the visit. Counting from 11/21/2026 would add one extra month. The correct earliest date, if the values stay up, is 4/21/2027.'}],
 teach:'Use the HIV columns of Table 2 together with the Notes. For MMR, a CD4 percentage below 15% or a count below 200 cells/mm3 is a contraindication (red). At 15% and 200 or above, MMR is recommended when the values have been that high for at least 6 months and there is no evidence of immunity, as 2 doses at least 4 weeks apart. Count the 6 months from the first date at or above both thresholds, 10/21/2026, which gives 4/21/2027. If the count falls below either threshold before then, the clock stops and MMR is contraindicated again.',
 cite:'AAFP-Adult-Schedule-2026.pdf Table 2 (MMR row, HIV columns) and Notes (MMR, HIV infection); 9/21 transcript (T: use the Notes)'},

{id:'sb22', skill:'apply', prof:'Dr. Parsons', multi:true, tier:'new', topic:'sbarp', sub:'sort', concept:'sbar-vaccine-background-immunocompromised-multi', img:'aafp_t2',
 tags:['SBAR','vaccine-needs SBAR','Background','immunocompromised'], source:'both',
 stem:'A pharmacy intern writes a vaccine-needs SBAR about a 40-year-old patient with lupus who takes an immunosuppressant and has no zoster vaccine on record. Which statements belong in the Background? Select all that apply.',
 options:[
  {t:'She is 40 years old, has lupus and takes an immunosuppressant', correct:true, why:'Slide 22 puts patient characteristics in the Background, as in Mrs. Jones’s age, occupation and type 2 diabetes, and (T) these are the characteristics that back up the assessment. Lupus treated with an immunosuppressant is the fact that places her in the immunocompromised column of Table 2.'},
  {t:'Her vaccine history shows no recombinant zoster vaccine, and her screening checklist was negative', correct:true, why:'Slide 22 lists the vaccine history and the screening checklist result in the Background. A missing zoster vaccine is history that the assessment relies on, and the checklist result tells the preceptor that no contraindication was found. Neither is a recommendation.'},
  {t:'AAFP recommends a 2-dose recombinant zoster series, 2–6 months apart, for immunocompromising conditions', correct:false, why:'On slide 22, “AAFP recommends PCV 21 for patients aged 19–64 with diabetes…” is the Assessment, because it states the source and what is indicated for the patient. This sentence has the same form for the zoster vaccine, so it is Assessment, not Background.'},
  {t:'I recommend Shingrix today, with the second dose in 2–6 months', correct:false, why:'Slide 22 puts “I recommend…” and the next-dose timing in the Recommendation. The 2–6 month interval for the second dose is part of that request, as in Mrs. Jones’s example, so this sentence is the Recommendation and not a patient characteristic.'},
  {t:'She is at the pharmacy to be assessed for vaccine needs', correct:false, why:'Slide 22 begins with a short Situation, that the patient is at the pharmacy to be assessed for vaccine needs, and (T) the situation should be something pretty simple. It names the reason for contact and not a characteristic of the patient, so it is not Background.'}],
 teach:'Vaccine-needs SBAR (Immunizations slide 22): Situation is a simple reason for contact; Background holds the patient characteristics and vaccine history that support the assessment; Assessment states the AAFP source and what is indicated; Recommendation lists what you recommend, including the next dose. (T) The lecture example was a 40-year-old with lupus on immunosuppressants who developed shingles because the vaccine was missed, which is why immunocompromised patients are checked first in Table 2. Sort each sentence by the job it does, not by the words it shares with another part.',
 cite:'Immunizations-Pre-Lab.pdf slides 20–22; 9/21 transcript (T); AAFP-Adult-Schedule-2026.pdf Table 2 and Notes (RZV)'},

{id:'sb23', skill:'tell', prof:'Dr. Parsons', tier:'new', topic:'sbarp', sub:'sort', concept:'sbar-two-versions',
 tags:['SBAR','two SBAR versions','Background vs Assessment'], source:'both',
 stem:'Two SBARs are written on the same day: one corrects a prescription error and one gives a vaccine recommendation. Which pairing of a sentence with its SBAR part is correct?',
 options:[
  {t:'In the prescription-error SBAR, “The maximum dose of ibuprofen is 3.2 g/day” is Background', correct:true, why:'Week 11 gives this exact sentence as its Background example for a prescription error: Background is the clinical facts needed to understand the situation, and the Assessment is the problem, that the total daily dose prescribed is too high.'},
  {t:'In the vaccine SBAR, “AAFP recommends Shingrix for adults 50 years and older” is Background', correct:false, why:'On slide 22 the Assessment states what AAFP recommends, as in “AAFP recommends PCV 21 for patients aged 19–64 with diabetes, Shingrix in patients over 50 years old…”. This sentence has the same form, so it is Assessment and not a patient characteristic.'},
  {t:'In the prescription-error SBAR, “The total daily dose prescribed is too high” is Background', correct:false, why:'Week 11 gives this sentence as its example of an Assessment: it states the actual problem. A Background sentence reports the clinical facts, such as the maximum dose. Placing the conclusion in the Background states the problem before the facts that support it.'},
  {t:'In the vaccine SBAR, “She is 61 years old with type 2 diabetes” is Assessment', correct:false, why:'Slide 22 puts the age, the occupation, the conditions and the vaccine history in the Background, and (T) the background is the characteristics that back up the assessment. A fact about the patient is not an interpretation of the schedule.'}],
 teach:'The four letters are the same, but the dividing line between Background and Assessment depends on the call. Prescription-error SBAR (Week 11): Background = clinical facts needed to understand the situation, such as the maximum dose; Assessment = the actual problem and why it matters. Vaccine-needs SBAR (Immunizations slide 22): Background = patient characteristics and vaccine history; Assessment = what the AAFP schedule recommends for that patient. Ask whether the sentence reports something known, or states what the guideline or the facts conclude, and which kind of call is being made.',
 cite:'APC-III-Week-11-OSCE-Prep_2026.pdf (Background vs Assessment); Immunizations-Pre-Lab.pdf slides 20–22; 9/21 transcript (T)'},

{id:'sb24', skill:'apply', prof:'Dr. Parsons', tier:'new', topic:'sbarp', sub:'rec', concept:'sbar-vaccine-assessment-laiv3-immunocompromised', img:'aafp_app1',
 tags:['SBAR','vaccine-needs SBAR','LAIV3','contraindication','immunocompromised'], source:'both',
 stem:'A 44-year-old patient with lupus takes an immunosuppressant and asks for FluMist (LAIV3) on 11/21/2026. Which Assessment is correct for the SBAR to the preceptor?',
 options:[
  {t:'LAIV3 is contraindicated in immunocompromised persons, so FluMist should not be given; an inactivated vaccine is recommended', correct:true, why:'The Appendix lists immunocompromise from any cause, including medications and HIV infection, as a LAIV3 contraindication, and Table 2 shows red for LAIV3 in the immunocompromised column and yellow for inactivated or recombinant influenza vaccine, 1 dose annually.'},
  {t:'LAIV3 is a precaution in immunocompromised persons, so FluMist may be given to this patient if the benefit outweighs the risk', correct:false, why:'A precaution is navy in Table 2 and allows a risk-benefit decision. For immunocompromised persons the LAIV3 cell is red, and the Appendix places immunocompromise under Contraindicated or Not Recommended. Footnote 1 says that when a contraindication is present the vaccine should not be given.'},
  {t:'LAIV3 is acceptable because the patient is 44 years old, within the 19–49 year range given for FluMist', correct:false, why:'The age range appears in the navy precaution cells for conditions such as diabetes. It does not apply to the immunocompromised column, which is red. Age cannot override a contraindication, and the Appendix lists immunocompromise from medications among the reasons not to give LAIV3.'},
  {t:'Influenza vaccination is deferred until the immunosuppressant is stopped, because the response would be weak and the benefit small', correct:false, why:'Table 2 shows yellow for inactivated or recombinant influenza vaccine in the immunocompromised column, 1 dose annually, so influenza vaccination is recommended. (T) The lecture example notes that when an immunosuppressant cannot be stopped, the vaccine is still given, with a possibly weaker response.'}],
 teach:'Immunocompromised patients are red for the live vaccines in Table 2 (LAIV3, MMR, varicella) and yellow or orange for most inactivated vaccines (influenza, COVID-19, Tdap, pneumococcal, zoster). The Appendix backs the color: LAIV3 is contraindicated in anyone immunocompromised due to any cause, including medications and HIV infection. A red cell means do not give; a navy cell means a precaution with a risk-benefit decision. In the Assessment, state the contraindication and cite the AAFP (T); in the Recommendation, give the inactivated option.',
 cite:'AAFP-Adult-Schedule-2026.pdf Table 2 (LAIV3 and influenza rows) and Appendix (LAIV3 contraindications; footnotes 1–2); 9/21 transcript (T)'}
);
